>> Thank you for unmuting me.

00:13:04.725 --> 00:13:05.845
Good evening.

00:13:06.645 --> 00:13:11.605
As you all know, we signed a contract last December for the new 2021

00:13:11.605 --> 00:13:13.205
town wide revaluation

00:13:13.205 --> 00:13:13.685
project.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:19.510
And that contract included performing full inspections of every property in town.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.750
Due to COVID, I think everybody knows that

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:28.950
there would be huge resistance as well as it's probably

00:13:29.415 --> 00:13:38.935
not the greatest idea to be asking to go into each and every home. Eric asked me to reach out to the vendor to see what the cost would be if we changed our contract

00:13:39.335 --> 00:13:42.695
to one in which we send a data mailer

00:13:42.695 --> 00:13:45.850
rather than go out to visit each property.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:53.610
I did reach out to the vendor. They responded with an email that I believe Eric said everybody should have a copy of tonight.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:58.570
The email they sent back said we could convert the project to a data mailer project.

00:13:59.315 --> 00:14:03.955
The cost would be the same. And if they had to inspect more than 200 parcels,

00:14:03.955 --> 00:14:08.595
that we would be charged $30 for each parcel over the 200 properties.

00:14:09.715 --> 00:14:21.170
Why would we inspect somebody if we were doing data mailers? Because if you send out data mailers and there's no response, you're required by law to then follow-up with an inspection of the property.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:29.805
So if you send out 1,000 data mailers and only 500 people responded, you could still be forced to go and visit the other 500 properties.

00:14:30.525 --> 00:14:35.245
So at this point, we kind of have a couple of options on how to go forward.

00:14:36.125 --> 00:14:37.085
2020

00:14:37.085 --> 00:14:38.765
revaluation projects

00:14:38.765 --> 00:14:40.525
are not completed

00:14:40.525 --> 00:14:42.685
as of right now and will be

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:43.780
by January

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:44.660
2021.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:49.300
So, those people were already under contract and they dealt with COVID as well.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.020
What did those towns do?

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:58.495
The the governor signed an executive order that that basically relieved them from

00:14:58.815 --> 00:15:01.535
performing the full inspection and

00:15:03.055 --> 00:15:06.255
different towns did different things. Some towns tried to convert

00:15:06.655 --> 00:15:14.200
or do data mailers for the inspection that they hadn't done. Because by the time COVID hit, it really sort of got rampant in like February.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.800
If you were doing a 2020

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.080
revaluation, you would have been close to done with

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:33.735
your data collection. In fact, our project is a 2021 Revel, and we would have liked to start data collection in July of this year and banged it out in July and August, but we didn't because of COVID.

00:15:33.975 --> 00:15:37.735
I talked with the vendor. We sat, and we waited to try and figure out what we were going to do.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:46.070
I've talked with the Office of Policy and Management several times, including today, to get another update. And I'm being told that

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:50.150
they expect the same executive order for 2021

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.870
revaluation projects as they had

00:15:53.155 --> 00:15:54.915
for 2020.

00:15:55.155 --> 00:15:56.355
While the governor,

00:15:57.155 --> 00:15:59.715
on the ninth extended all existing,

00:16:01.155 --> 00:16:02.435
executive orders,

00:16:02.595 --> 00:16:13.730
the the executive order was only for twenty twenty towns and not twenty twenty one towns. So we're expecting a new executive order that's just for 2021

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.010
and will basically

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.250
relieve us of that requirement.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:24.865
So, what we're left with is what do we do with the contract? We have a couple of options and

00:16:25.585 --> 00:16:28.145
we need some guidance on how to go forward.

00:16:29.025 --> 00:16:30.065
At this point,

00:16:30.625 --> 00:16:34.385
we still could we could convert to the data mailer

00:16:35.105 --> 00:16:36.065
process,

00:16:36.225 --> 00:16:49.790
but I'm not sure that that's the the best bet. We also have the option to still send the data collector out to every house and not try to get in. Obviously, we have to do some public relations and inform the town through

00:16:50.670 --> 00:17:02.795
press releases as well as the web page on what we're doing, but we could still send a data collector up and down every street to every house. They could ring the doorbell, hand them a sheet of paper, tell them who they are, just walk around the outside of the property,

00:17:03.035 --> 00:17:04.395
confirm the measurements,

00:17:04.395 --> 00:17:15.990
confirm that the house is still the same, the square footage is the same. They'd be looking for things like central air decks, pools, finished area over the garage. They see curtains. They could bang on the door again and ask a question about that.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:26.105
So that is one option where they would do that, and they would not try to get inside the property, but they would go out and visit every single property and confirm everything that's on the card.

00:17:26.345 --> 00:17:28.665
Or the option b is to,

00:17:28.825 --> 00:17:37.480
you know, send a data mailer in in which we send out to the people a card, it tells them what we have for data on their property record.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:59.805
And they confirm that it's accurate, or they let us know if if something is different and needs to be changed or or added and modified. And on those ones that claim there's something different, we have to follow those up with an inspection. So we would have to go out to anybody who didn't respond or anybody who responded with data that was contrary to what the town had. Well, tell us there's an option c, please.

00:18:00.365 --> 00:18:01.965
What would option c be?

00:18:02.765 --> 00:18:14.270
>> Move it to next year when there's no COVID issue. And I don't think data mailers are a good solution. Nobody's gonna come out and say, yeah, I I may have renovated my kitchen and put in $50. We're good. No one's gonna admit that.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.190
You know? I mean,

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:22.655
we see it all the time. We go and do work on a house and it's it's not it's not been updated. You know, we know it's not. So,

00:18:23.935 --> 00:18:26.175
I I just don't see that as a good solution.

00:18:26.495 --> 00:18:33.250
I don't see the data mill as a good solution and, you know, a lot of things that you people do aren't visual from the outside.

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:36.210
New bathrooms, new kitchens,

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.530
those types of upgrades that do add true value,

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:41.810
we're not gonna catch.

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:45.010
I feel like that's a waste of funds to do a huge contract,

00:18:45.635 --> 00:18:49.715
meet our statutory requirement, and then not really gain from it.

00:18:50.275 --> 00:18:53.715
I mean, unless I'm missing something, John. I I have you had a different experience with them?

00:18:54.755 --> 00:19:11.260
>> Well, no. I mean, I can only offer you the options that are available. There were 35 towns that did revaluation last year. If they had allowed all of those to put it off for one year and then we got to 2021, they said, oh, let's have them all put it off again. There's another 35 towns. Now you're looking at 101

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:13.660
